# CLOUDFLARE PAGES HEADER RULES. Copied verbatim into dist/ by Vite; Cloudflare Pages reads
# `_headers` from the root of the deployed output and never serves the file itself.
#
# Two jobs, and they are separate: the first block is what the browser is allowed to do, the
# rest is how long the CDN and the browser may keep each kind of file.
#
# ═══ THE CONTENT-SECURITY-POLICY WAS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED ═══
#
# A policy that is one directive too tight breaks the product silently — the page renders and the
# numbers are simply missing. So the allowed origins below were read out of the BUILT output
# (`grep` over dist/assets for every https:// host it contains) rather than reasoned about, and
# the whole policy was then served over the real build and the calculator driven in a browser
# with the console open. What it needs and why:
#
#   img-src   ddragon.leagueoflegends.com — every champion portrait, ability icon, item icon and
#             rune icon is hotlinked from Data Dragon at a patch-numbered path
#             (src/ui/data/roster.ts). Remove this host and every icon on the site disappears.
#   style-src 'unsafe-inline' — React writes `style` attributes for the burndown's geometry, and
#             CSP treats a style ATTRIBUTE as inline style. Without it the chart loses its
#             layout. No inline <style> block ships; this is the attribute case only.
#   script-src 'self' with NO 'unsafe-inline' — Vite emits external module scripts only, so the
#             strictest useful setting costs nothing here. If a third-party script is ever added
#             (analytics, advertising, error monitoring), THIS LINE IS WHAT WILL BLOCK IT, and
#             that is the intended behaviour: it should be a deliberate edit, not a surprise.
#   connect-src 'self' — the only network calls the app makes are to /data/*.json on its own
#             origin. Nothing is sent anywhere.
#   font-src  'self' data: — AND THIS IS THE ONE THE MEASUREMENT CAUGHT. Vite inlines any asset
#             under 4 KB into the stylesheet as a base64 `data:` URI, and nine of the smaller
#             font subsets (Saira, IBM Plex Sans and JetBrains Mono) come in under that. With
#             `font-src 'self'` alone the browser blocked all nine and the page fell back to
#             system faces — no error a reader would understand, just the wrong typography. A
#             `data:` font is bytes already inside a stylesheet this policy trusted, so allowing
#             the scheme grants nothing new.
#
# frame-ancestors 'none' does the job X-Frame-Options does, for browsers that honour CSP; the
# older header is kept for the ones that do not.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' https://ddragon.leagueoflegends.com; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Vite content-hashes every file in /assets/, so a given URL's contents can never change. A year
# is the conventional maximum and `immutable` tells the browser not even to revalidate.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# THE PUBLISHED DATA IS NOT HASHED, and it is the one thing here that must never be stale: a
# corrected damage figure lands in these files under the SAME name. Five minutes at the browser,
# and `must-revalidate` so the browser asks rather than guesses once that expires.
/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate
